  <h1>Oliver and the Ocean</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p><em>I have lived a good life. I want Oliver to know that. I want him to know that the smallness was my choice, and I would make it again. I want him to stop blaming himself for the things he couldn't give me. I want him to know that the ocean was never the point. He was the point. He has always been the point.</em></p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p><em>Tell him if you can. Tell him I'm not sorry. Tell him I was never sorry.</em></p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Oliver closed the notebook. He sat at the kitchen table for a long time. The house was quiet. The photographs were on the wall. Eleanor at the Grand Canyon. Eleanor in the desert. Eleanor in the mountains.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She had been to the ocean without him. He knew that now. He knew she'd gone with friends, with coworkers, with her sister on the one trip he'd let her take alone. She had seen it. She had loved it. And she had come home and never once let him feel that he had failed her by being who he was.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He called Rose. "I read it," he said.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"And?"</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"She was happy. Most of the time. She made peace with things. She didn't blame me."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"I never thought she blamed you, Dad."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"I know. But I needed to know."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He was quiet for a moment. Then: "Next summer. The beach house. I want to come again."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"We'd love that."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"I'll come for the whole two weeks."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"You don't have to — "</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"I want to. I want to sit on the deck. I want to watch the kids play. I want to be there."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"Okay, Dad."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He hung up. He sat in the kitchen for a while longer. The notebook was closed on the table. The sun was going down outside, painting the walls the way it always did at this hour, and the light was the same light that Eleanor used to eat breakfast in, all those winters ago.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He got up and made dinner. He stood at the counter to eat it. He washed the dishes and dried them and put them away.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Then he went to the closet, in the bedroom, and found the box of letters — the small notes Eleanor had left him over the years, the ones that weren't love letters but were something else, something he now understood better than he had before.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He read two of them. Then he closed the box and put it back on the shelf.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He went to bed early. He slept without dreaming.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
